{
  "result": "The old lighthouse stood at the edge of the harbor where the sea met the cliffs in a constant argument of wind and stone. Every evening, Elias climbed the spiral stairs, carrying oil, matches, and a notebook filled with weather observations. Some nights the fog arrived like a guest, hiding the water and muffling the sound of waves against the rocks. On those nights the light became the only compass for sailors who were lost and frightened.\n\nElias did not consider his work heroic. He liked the routine, the quiet, and the feeling that his actions mattered to strangers he would never meet. In the winter he mended the iron door and watched gulls fight over scraps near the dock. In the spring he planted herbs in the salt hardened soil beside the tower and traded them for bread with fishermen from the village below. When storms threatened, he checked the ropes, secured the shutters, and listened to the radio for warnings from the mainland.\n\nPeople sometimes asked why he stayed alone on such a remote point of land. He answered that solitude taught him patience and attention. He learned to hear changes in the wind before they became visible, to notice a loosened bolt before it failed, and to appreciate the return of stars after long cloudy weeks. The lighthouse was more than a building. It was a promise kept in darkness, a steady rhythm against uncertainty, and a reminder that care, repeated daily, can guide others safely home.",
